<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armando Diaz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Armando Diaz was an Italian general who became chief of staff of the army in November 1917, after the defeat at Caporetto. He rebuilt morale, held the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a> in 1918 and directed the victory of Vittorio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> that ended the war on the Italian front. He was made a marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s first minister of war from 1922 to 1924.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armando-diaz.jpg" alt="Armando Diaz" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mario Nunes Vais · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armando_Diaz_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Armando Diaz was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 5 December 1861, the son of Lodovico, a navy officer, and Irene Cecconi, the daughter of a minor noble. The family had some distant Spanish ancestry, most likely from the years of the Crown of Aragon. He began as a cadet at the Military College of Naples and graduated as an artillery officer from the Military College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1884. In 1894 he attended the School of War and finished first in his class, then worked for two years in the office of General Alberto Pollio. As a colonel he served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a>, commanding the 21st and then the 93rd Infantry, and he was wounded at Zanzur in 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Diaz was assigned to the high command as head of operations under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luigi-cadorna">Luigi Cadorna</a>. Promoted to two-star general in June 1916, he commanded the 49th division and then the 23rd Army Corps.</p><p>After the disaster at Caporetto in October 1917, he was called on 8 November to succeed Cadorna as chief of the general staff. He recovered what remained of the army and organised resistance on the Monte Grappa massif and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a>, which halted the Austro-Hungarian offensive in the First Battle of Monte Grappa. In the summer of 1918 he oversaw the victory in the Battle of the Piave River, and later that year he led 1.4 million Italian troops at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vittorio-veneto">Battle of Vittorio Veneto</a>, which ended the war on the Italian front. He announced the victory in his Bollettino della Vittoria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war Diaz became a senator. In 1921 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> made him the first Duke of the Victory, and in the same year he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he attended the groundbreaking of the Liberty Memorial in Kansas City alongside Foch and Pershing. Benito Mussolini named him Minister of War, and on his retirement in 1924 he received the honour of Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1928 and was buried in the church of Santa Maria degli Angeli e dei Martiri. Admiral Paolo Thaon di Revel was interred next to him in 1948.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armando Diaz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1904" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Armando Diaz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1904">What was here in 1904? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
